makeHomotopies -- returns a system of higher homotopies
*******************************************************

  * Usage: 
        H = makeHomotopies(f,F,b)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, 1xn matrix of elements of S
      * F, a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, admitting homotopies for the
        entries of f
      * b, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, how far back to compute the
        homotopies (defaults to length of F)
  * Outputs:
      * H, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, gives the higher
        homotopy from F_i corresponding to a monomial with exponent vector L as
        the value $H#\{L,i\}$

Description
===========

Given a $1\times n$ matrix f, and a chain complex F, the script attempts to
make a family of higher homotopies on F for the elements of f, in the sense
described, for example, in Eisenbud "Enriched Free Resolutions and Change of
Rings".

The output is a hash table with entries of the form $\{J,i\}=>s$, where J is a
list of non-negative integers, of length n and $H\#\{J,i\}: F_i->F_{i+2|J|-1}$
are maps satisfying the conditions $$ H\#\{e0,i\} = d; $$ and $$
H#\{e0,i+1\}*H#\{e,i\}+H#\{e,i-1\}H#\{e0,i\} = f_i, $$ where $e0 =
\{0,\dots,0\}$ and $e$ is the index of degree 1 with a 1 in the $i$-th place;
and, for each index list I with |I|<=d, $$ sum_{J<I} H#\{I\setminus J, \}
H#\{J, \} = 0. $$

To make the maps homogeneous, $H\#\{J,i\}$ is actually a map from a an
appropriate negative twist of F to a shift of S.

